Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a good time to get organized. I won’t share any of this with anyone unless you ask me to. A few quick clarifying questions before I draft a tailored checklist or email for your attorney:
- Any children or custody/support issues involved?
- Any temporary orders already in place (support, custody, stay-away)?
- Has the other side made any settlement offer yet?
- Any high-value or complicated assets (business, retirement accounts, real estate out of state)?

Below is a compact, usable plan you can take to your attorney and to the status conference.

1) What to expect at the status conference (Fulton County civil division)
- Judge will usually want: case status, whether discovery is completed or on schedule, whether parties have discussed settlement/mediation, any pending motions, a proposed schedule (deadlines, trial date), and whether temporary issues remain unresolved.
- Be prepared to confirm whether you want mediation, need more time for discovery, or seek any temporary orders.

2) Documents to bring and prepare
- Court papers you’ve already received/served (complaint, answer, motions, notices).
- Any settlement offers/correspondence (emails, letters, texts).
- Financial records (last 3 years tax returns, recent pay stubs, W-2s/1099s).
- Bank statements (checking/savings), investment and retirement account statements.
- Mortgage statements, deeds, vehicle titles/registration, loan statements, credit card statements.
- Business financials (P&L, balance sheets) if business ownership exists.
- Valuations or appraisals (real estate, business, vehicles) if available.
- Proofs of monthly expenses (bills, childcare, insurance, medical).
- Records of support/childcare expenses and receipts.
- Documents showing contributions to property (large purchases, renovations).
- Communications relevant to the case (texts, emails) and any evidence of misconduct if relevant.
- Witness list and contact info (friends, family, professionals).
- Previously prepared declarations/affidavits or expert reports.

3) Key questions to ask your attorney about settlement vs. trial
A. Case assessment
- Based on the facts and evidence, what is our realistic best-case and worst-case outcome at trial?
- What are the main legal strengths and weaknesses of my case?
- How do local judges in Fulton County typically handle cases like mine?

B. Settlement valuation & strategy
- What would be a reasonable settlement range for my goals (specific numbers or ranges for property split, alimony, child support/visitation)?
- What terms should I prioritize (e.g., custody schedule, spousal support duration, who keeps the house)?
- How would you negotiate — do we set firm bottom-line limits or remain flexible?
- If I accept a settlement, what protections should be included (enforcement provisions, tax language, non-disparagement, confidentiality)?

C. Risks, costs, and timing
- What is the estimated cost (fees, experts, court costs) and timeline to take this to trial?
- What non-monetary costs should I expect (time, stress, children’s exposure)?
- How likely is an appeal and how would that affect me?

D. Evidence & discovery
- What evidence or documents will most impact the judge’s decision?
- What additional discovery should we pursue (depositions, subpoenas, forensic accounting)?
- Do we need expert witnesses (forensic accountant, valuation expert, custody evaluator)?

E. Pre-trial posture and motions
- Are there dispositive motions or temporary relief we should file now (temporary support, exclusive use of home, restraining order)?
- What would a trial-ready schedule look like, and when should we lock in a trial date?

F. Settlement mechanics
- If settlement is reached, how long does the court take to approve/enter final judgment?
- Should settlement be a consent order or be incorporated into final judgment? (Pros/cons)
- How do we handle confidentiality and enforcement language?

G. Communication & decision process
- How will you consult me about offers — do you recommend immediate acceptance, counteroffers, or running them by me first?
- What is your estimate of the probability we will do better at trial than a fair settlement?
- If I want to settle only on certain terms, how will you secure those terms?

4) Practical checklist for the next 2 months (until status conference)
- Provide attorney with all documents above ASAP (organized and labeled).
- Ask attorney to prepare a proposed scheduling order for the status conference.
- Decide whether you want to pursue mediation before the status conference; ask attorney about mediation options and costs.
- Identify any immediate temporary orders needed and request motions if required.
- Discuss possible experts and obtain quotes if needed.
- Prepare a concise one-page summary of your goals/priorities for the lawyer to present at conference.

5) How I can help right now
- Draft a one-page summary of your case and goals to give to Robert Kim.
- Draft a concise email to Robert Kim listing the questions above and asking for his assessment before the status conference.
- Create a printable checklist of documents to bring.

Tell me which of those you’d like me to do (draft the email, draft the one-page summary, or create the printable checklist), and answer the clarifying questions above if you can — then I’ll prepare the document.

