I can help — below is a focused, practical checklist and set of questions you can take to Robert Kim (or use in a meeting/email) to decide whether to accept a settlement or push for trial, plus a short document checklist and immediate next steps before the status conference.

If you want, I can also turn this into a polished email to Robert Kim or a one-page checklist you can print. Which would you prefer?

1) Brief case summary to give your attorney (one page)
- Case type & court: Divorce, Fulton County, GA — civil division.
- Your attorney: Robert Kim, Kim & Associates.
- Opposing party: respondent.
- Key dates: status conference in two months.
- High-level objectives: what you absolutely need (e.g., custody arrangement, minimum financial support, keep home) and what you’re willing to trade.
- Short factual timeline of major events (marriage date, separation date, major exchanges/transactions, children/birthdates if relevant).

2) Questions to ask your attorney about settlement vs trial
A. Case strength & likely outcomes
- Based on the facts and evidence we have, what are the realistic outcomes at trial (range of likely court orders for property division, alimony, custody/support if applicable)?
- What are the strongest and weakest points of our case?
- What are the biggest legal hurdles or uncertainty factors in Fulton County/GA law for our situation?

B. Settlement value & negotiation strategy
- What is a reasonable settlement range from our perspective? (Best-case / likely / worst-case)
- What would be the minimum acceptable settlement you recommend (my “walk-away” point)?
- Which issues should we prioritize in settlement (e.g., custody, spousal support, home, retirement accounts)?
- Should we use mediation? If so, when and who will pay for it?
- How do we protect non-financial concerns (custody schedule, parenting time, confidentiality) in a settlement?

C. Costs, timing, and risks of going to trial
- Estimated timeline to trial if we don’t settle (discovery schedule, pretrial dates, how long the trial itself might take).
- Estimated attorney fees and other costs (experts, appraisals, deposition costs). Break down best/worst case.
- What are the key risks of going to trial (unpredictable judge rulings, appeals, enforcement issues)?
- How long after trial would the final decree be entered and enforceable?

D. Evidence, discovery, and witnesses
- What specific evidence or documentation would most strengthen our case?
- Should we plan depositions of the respondent or witnesses? Who?
- Do we need expert witnesses (forensic accountant, custody evaluator, business valuation, mental health)?
- Are there likely pretrial motions (for sanctions, protective orders, excluding evidence)?

E. Settlement terms & enforceability
- If we settle, how do we structure clear, enforceable terms (QDR/qualified domestic relations order for pensions, timelines for transfer of property, contingencies)?
- How do we handle tax consequences of asset division and support?
- How will enforcement work if the other party violates the agreement? (contempt, garnishment, liens)
- Can the settlement include confidentiality/non-disparagement clauses? Are they enforceable here?

F. Temporary/Interim orders & immediate protections
- Should we seek any temporary orders before the status conference (temporary support, exclusive use of home, restraining orders)?
- Do any urgent steps need to be taken now to protect assets (e.g., freeze accounts, change beneficiaries, secure documents)?

G. Settlement offers & deadlines
- If an offer is presented before the status conference, how quickly can you evaluate it and advise me?
- If settlement intentionally delays beyond status conference, what effect does that have on scheduling or court pressure?

H. Client role & communication
- What information or decisions will you need from me and on what timeline?
- How will you keep me informed (email/phone)? What is the expected response time during negotiation windows?

3) Documents and evidence to bring / gather
Financial documents
- Recent pay stubs (last 3–6 months), W-2s/1099s (last 2–3 years), tax returns.
- Bank statements for all accounts (last 12–24 months).
- Credit card statements and personal loan/line-of-credit statements.
- Mortgage statements, deeds, titles, vehicle registrations.
- Retirement account statements (401(k), IRAs), brokerage statements.
- Business financials if one or both parties own businesses (P&L, balance sheet, tax returns).
- Appraisals or valuation reports (real estate, business).

Legal/communication documents
- Any prior court filings, temporary orders, or settlement offers.
- Written communications relevant to the case (emails, texts, letters).
- Contracts (pre-nup, post-nup), separation agreements, promissory notes.

Parenting/custody evidence (if applicable)
- Child custody schedules, school records, medical records, childcare expenses.
- Any court-ordered evaluations or CPS reports.
- Documentation of parenting activities/time with children (calendar, photos, messages).

Other evidence
- Photographs, receipts, proof of payments/transfers.
- Witness names and contact info with brief notes on what they can confirm.

4) What to expect at the status conference
- Ask: What does the judge typically expect at a Fulton County status conference for divorce cases?
- Typical items: scheduling order (discovery deadlines), setting deadlines for mediation, identifying outstanding motions, trial date window, and required filings (pretrial statements).
- Ask your attorney what they will request at the status conference (extensions, discovery plan, set mediation, temporary orders).

5) How to decide whether to accept a settlement — practical criteria
- Compare settlement value to your realistic trial outcome range (financial, custody, timelines).
- Factor in non-monetary priorities (privacy, speed, emotional cost, certainty).
- Compare costs and time: how much will trial likely cost in money and stress vs benefit of potentially better outcome.
- Consider enforceability: a guaranteed enforceable settlement can be worth more than a possibly better but uncertain trial result.
- Judge’s unpredictability and appellate possibilities: would a small improvement at trial justify the risk/cost?

6) Immediate next steps you can take (today / this week)
- Create the one-page case summary above and send it to Robert Kim.
- Gather the high-priority documents listed and make organized folders (digital + printed).
- Make a short list of your top 3 objectives and top 3 absolute deal-breakers to discuss with counsel.
- Ask Robert for an estimated fee projection for going to trial vs mediating/settling now.
- Decide if you want me to draft a concise email to Robert with these questions and your case summary.

7) Offer to help
- I can:
  - Draft a concise email to Robert Kim listing these questions and attaching your case summary.
  - Create a printable one-page checklist you can bring to the meeting.
  - Organize your document list into a simple folder structure with filenames.
  - Draft suggested settlement language items to discuss (e.g., timing, QDRO language, custody specifics)—if you want.

Would you like me to draft the email to Robert Kim that includes these questions and your case summary? If yes, tell me:
- Any additional facts you want included in the one-page summary (marriage/separation dates, children, major assets),
- Whether you want the attorney asked about an estimated fee range for trial vs settlement.

