Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — but first a quick note: I’m not a lawyer. I can help you organize questions, evidence, and decision factors to discuss with Robert Kim so you can make an informed choice about settlement vs. trial. Use this to guide your conversation with him; follow his legal advice for your case.

Suggested questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
- Case status and timeline
  - What is the purpose of the status conference and what will the judge likely ask or order?
  - What deadlines will be set at the status conference (discovery cutoff, motions deadlines, pretrial conference, trial date)?
  - What is a realistic timeline to resolution if we settle vs. if we go to trial?
- Strengths, weaknesses, and likely outcomes
  - What are the strongest and weakest parts of my case?
  - Based on your experience and the judge assigned (if known), what are realistic trial outcomes or ranges (e.g., property split, alimony, custody/support)?
  - What are the biggest risks if we go to trial?
- Settlement evaluation
  - If there’s a settlement offer (or if I make one), how should we value it? What factors should I consider (net after costs/fees, tax consequences, enforceability)?
  - What minimum terms would you recommend I accept, and what are “deal-breakers” to reject?
  - Would a settlement include confidentiality, release language, or waivers I should be concerned about?
- Costs and resources
  - What are estimated attorney fees to trial (best-case / worst-case)?
  - Are there expected extra costs for experts, appraisals, depositions, or investigators?
  - If we settle now, how much could I realistically save in fees and time?
- Evidence, witnesses, and discovery
  - What specific documents and records will the court expect or ask for in discovery for Fulton County divorce cases?
  - Which witnesses would be important at trial? Are there experts we should consider (valuation, forensic accountant, child psychologist)?
  - How should we preserve evidence (texts, emails, photos, bank records)? Any concerns about authenticity or admissibility?
  - Will we need subpoenas for third‑party records (banks, employers, schools)?
- Custody and support (if applicable)
  - How will custody/visitation proposals be evaluated? What documentation about the children (school, medical, schedules) will help?
  - How is child support calculated here and what documents show income and expenses?
- Settlement mechanics and enforcement
  - How do we draft enforceable settlement terms (installments, property transfers, liens, contempt remedies)?
  - If the other side breaches a settlement, what’s the enforcement process and expected costs/time?
- Strategy and negotiation
  - What negotiation strategy do you recommend given the facts? (e.g., aggressive trial posture, mediation first, limited-purpose settlement)
  - Should we try mediation or another ADR before the status conference or after discovery?
  - If we reject an offer, what bargaining points should we push to improve a later offer?
- Local procedure and judge preferences
  - Are there Fulton County local rules or judge-specific practices I should know about? Any required forms or standard orders?
  - What should I expect at the courtroom (timing, dress, how hearings run)?
- Next steps and communication
  - What should I prepare before the status conference? What should I bring to our next meeting?
  - How frequently will you update me and what’s the best way to communicate (email, phone, portal)?

Documents and materials to gather and bring to your attorney
- Identification and case documents: Summons/petition/complaint, any orders already filed, case number, judge assigned.
- Financial records (last 3–5 years if available)
  - Pay stubs, W-2s, tax returns, 1099s
  - Bank and investment account statements
  - Retirement account statements, pension info
  - Mortgage statements, deeds, titles, vehicle registrations
  - Credit card statements, outstanding loan documents, debts
  - Business financials (if you or spouse own a business): profit & loss, balance sheets
- Household/expense evidence
  - Monthly bills, proof of utilities, childcare, medical expenses, school costs
- Communication and behavior evidence
  - Relevant emails, texts, social media posts, voicemails, photos, screenshots (with dates)
- Children-related records (if applicable)
  - School records, medical records, custody-related notes, schedules, any incidents or professional notes
- Agreements and legal documents
  - Prenuptial/postnuptial agreements, prior separation agreements, prior court orders
- Witness contact list and short summary of their expected testimony
- Any appraisals, property valuations, or valuations you have
- Chronology of important events (one-page timeline is helpful)
- Any offers or settlement proposals already exchanged (written offers, emails)

What to prepare before the status conference (practical checklist)
- Create a one-page summary for your lawyer: key facts, top objectives, non-negotiables, and immediate concerns.
- Compile the documentation list above and highlight critical items.
- Prepare a short chronology (dates and one-line descriptions).
- Note any settlement offers already made and what you liked/disliked about them.
- Write down your priorities (e.g., custody first, preserve retirement, stay in home) and what you absolutely cannot accept.
- Decide who should attend the status conference (usually the attorney attends; ask if your presence is required).
- Ask your attorney to prepare a proposed case-management schedule to present to the court, if appropriate.

How to evaluate a settlement offer (framework)
- Financial: what you actually receive after taxes and fees; present value vs. future streams.
- Certainty vs. risk: settlement gives certainty; trial has risk of getting less or more.
- Time and emotional cost: trial often takes months/years and is stressful.
- Enforcement: is the settlement easily enforceable (cash payments, property transfers, liens)?
- Non-financial terms: custody, visitation, school decisions, move-away restrictions, confidentiality.
- Future changes: alimony/support modifications possibilities; how stable is the income source?

Practical conversation starters (short questions you can read at your meeting)
- “Given the facts, what outcome at trial should I realistically expect?”
- “What would you recommend as a starting settlement demand and a bottom-line number?”
- “What are the biggest risks if we decline to settle and go to trial?”
- “How much more will this case likely cost if we go to trial?”
- “What specific discovery or evidence could change the valuation of this case?”
- “Should we propose mediation or try to settle directly?”

Other recommendations
- Consider mediation before incurring full trial costs — ask your attorney if a mediator experienced in family law in Fulton County would be helpful.
- Keep an organized folder (physical or secure digital) with the documents above and back it up.
- Don’t share sensitive posts on social media while the case is active. Assume anything posted could be used in court.
- If you’re unsure about your attorney’s recommendations, ask for a second opinion on settlement terms or case value.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a concise email to Robert Kim listing these questions and asking for a meeting before the status conference?
- Create a printable one-page checklist/summary you can bring to your attorney?
- Help draft a timeline or an initial settlement demand/counteroffer outline (if you have any offers)?

Tell me which of those you want, and any additional case details (children, major assets, existing offers) if you want a tailored draft.

