Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — below is a compact, practical guide you can use to organize for your attorney meeting and the upcoming status conference, plus a ready-to-use set of questions to ask Robert Kim. I’m not a lawyer, so treat this as organization and planning help; your attorney can give specific legal advice.

Quick framing — key tradeoffs (settlement vs trial)
- Settlement: more control, quicker resolution, lower cost, confidential, but may mean compromise and possibly less-than-ideal outcome.  
- Trial: chance to get best legal outcome if facts/law favor you, but higher cost, longer time, uncertain result, public record, emotional strain.  
Ask your attorney to estimate likely outcomes and costs so you can compare the expected value and risks of each path.

Questions to ask your attorney (use these at your meeting or call)
1. What is our realistic best-case, worst-case, and most likely outcome if we go to trial?  
2. What do you recommend: push for settlement or prepare for trial — and why?  
3. If we settle, what are reasonable settlement ranges or terms to expect? What concessions are typical?  
4. What is the estimated timeline (next steps) to trial if we don’t settle? When might a trial date be set?  
5. What are the projected legal fees and out-of-pocket costs to get to trial (discovery, experts, depositions, court fees)?  
6. What evidence or documents are strongest for our case? What are our weaknesses?  
7. Who are the key witnesses we would need for trial? How strong are their expected testimonies?  
8. Will we need expert witnesses (valuation, forensic accountant, custody evaluator)? Estimated costs?  
9. What discovery will be necessary (interrogatories, document requests, depositions)? How long will that take?  
10. Are there required mediation or settlement conferences before trial in Fulton County civil/divorce cases? If so, what is the process?  
11. What motions (temporary relief, motions in limine, summary judgment) should we expect and when?  
12. What specifically will happen at the status conference the judge set (procedural steps, deadlines, scheduling)?  
13. If we settle, how will enforcement be handled? Are post-judgment remedies adequate?  
14. Tax or financial consequences of settlement options (e.g., asset transfers, alimony) — should we consult a tax advisor?  
15. What authority will you need from me to accept a settlement (a dollar amount or specific terms)? Do you recommend setting a strict limit?  
16. What steps should I take immediately to strengthen the case or support settlement negotiations?  
17. What are the confidentiality options (sealed agreements, nondisclosure clauses) in settlement?  
18. How often will you update me and what is the best way to communicate (email/phone)? What is your fee structure for trial work vs settlement negotiation?

Documents and evidence checklist to gather
- Identification and case basics: court case number, judge’s name, calendar entry for status conference.  
- Financial: last 3 years of tax returns, recent pay stubs, W-2s/1099s, bank statements (6–12 months), retirement account statements, investment statements, mortgage/loan statements, deeds/titles, business financials if applicable.  
- Debts and liabilities: credit card statements, loan agreements, outstanding balances.  
- Property valuations: appraisals, real estate listings, vehicle titles.  
- Communications: emails, text messages, social media messages, letters relevant to the dispute (preserve originals and date/time).  
- Parenting/custody (if relevant): calendars, school records, medical records, childcare expenses, any police reports or CPS records.  
- Agreements/documents: prenuptial/postnuptial agreements, separation agreements, prior court orders.  
- Evidence of expenses/support: childcare receipts, medical bills, household bills.  
- Witness list and short summary of what each witness would say.  
- Chronology: one-page timeline of major events and dates relevant to the case.

What to prepare for the status conference (practical)
- Ask your attorney: what the judge typically does at a status conference in this division (scheduling, discovery deadlines, referral to mediation, setting motion deadlines).  
- Bring: case chronology, list of outstanding discovery items, proposed schedule (if your attorney wants one), and any immediate motions your side plans to file.  
- Be ready to confirm contact info, decide on proposed deadlines, and whether the parties agree to mediation or other alternative dispute resolution.

One-page case summary to give your attorney (use this at the meeting)
- Case caption/court/case number.  
- Goal: (what you want: divorce, property split, custody, support, etc.).  
- Key facts (bullet timeline, 6–10 lines).  
- Main assets/liabilities (brief list with approximate values).  
- Top 3 strengths of your case.  
- Top 3 weaknesses/exposure.  
- Urgent issues (temporary support, restraints, protective orders).  
- What settlement terms would be acceptable (your bottom-line items).

Practical tips
- Create a secure folder (digital + physical) with copies of everything; keep originals safe.  
- Preserve communications; consider saving them in PDF or screenshot with timestamps.  
- Track all expenses related to the case (for support/trial evidence).  
- Limit unilateral public statements (social media) about the case.  
- Ask about mediation — it’s often required or advisable and can dramatically lower cost/time.  
- Discuss emotional and logistical considerations (children, job, housing) with your attorney — they affect what settlement makes sense.

Sample short email you can send to Robert Kim (I can draft/send if you want)
Subject: Questions & preparation before status conference — [Case Name / Case No.]
Body (suggested):
Hi Robert — The judge set a status conference in two months. I’d like to schedule a meeting to discuss whether to pursue settlement or prepare for trial and to confirm what we need to have ready for the status conference. Attached is a one-page case summary and a list of documents I’m assembling. Please advise your availability and any immediate items you need from me. I’d also like your assessment of: likely outcomes at trial, estimated costs to go to trial, and a recommended settlement range if appropriate. Thank you, [Your name]

Next steps I can do for you
- Draft that email and either save as a draft or send it (tell me which).  
- Create the one-page case summary for you based on answers you give me.  
- Make a printable checklist of documents for you to gather.  
Tell me which of the above you want me to do, or if you want the email drafted now.

