- Certainty vs risk: settlement gives certainty; trial gives chance of better result but with risk of worse outcome
- Time and cost: trials are expensive and lengthy (attorney fees, expert fees, time off work)
- Emotional toll and publicity: trials can be stressful and public
- Enforcement and compliance: some settlements include protections (escrow, security) that judges’ orders may not provide as readily
- Flexibility of terms: settlements can craft creative solutions (gradual buyouts, structured payments)
- Precedent & finality: trial result may set record; settlement can include confidentiality and release of claims

D. Status conference and near-term checklist (what to prepare/expect)
- Ask attorney whether a joint status report or proposed scheduling order is required and whether you should file it
- Confirm discovery plan: what documents must be exchanged and any depositions to schedule
- Prepare an issues list for the court: narrow the contested matters and identify those suitable for settlement
- Prepare a proposed timeline: mediation date, expert disclosure deadlines, dispositive motions, trial month windows
- Prepare preliminary witness and exhibit lists (even if rough)
- Confirm whether judge will encourage mediation or issue deadlines at the status conference
- Bring to the meeting with your lawyer: prioritized goals, settlement bottom line, and any time-sensitive needs (e.g., housing, safety)
- Consider whether to request temporary relief (support, exclusive occupancy) if needed immediately

E. Practical prep steps for your meeting with Robert Kim
- Create a one-page “case summary” with timeline of key events, your goals/priority list (top 3), and a short list of questions above
- Send the documents listed in B electronically before your meeting so your attorney can review
- Decide your settlement threshold and any protected “non-negotiables”
- Ask your attorney for an estimated budget: likely total fees to mediation vs likely fees to trial
- Consider whether you want an initial neutral (mediator) evaluation to test settlement range

Next step
